"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang tells Trump 'we're not going to let [an AI slowdown] happen' | TechCrunch (https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/14/nvidia-ceo-jensen-huang-tells-trump-were-not-going-to-let-an-ai-slowdown-happen/)\nciteturn792view0 [wordlim: 25] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn791search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 220\nL0:   * cite0†Latest L1:   * cite1†Startups L2:   * cite2†Venture L3:   * cite3†Apple L4:   * cite4†Security L5:   * cite5†AI L6:   * cite6†Apps L7:   * cite7†Disrupt 2026 L8: \nL9:   * cite8†Events L10:   * cite9†Podcasts L11:   * cite10†Newsletters L12: \nL13: [Input: Search...][Button: Submit]\nL14: ### Topics\nL15: \nL16: cite0†Latest L17: \nL18: cite5†AI L19: \nL20: cite11†Amazon L21: \nL22: cite6†Apps L23: \nL24: cite12†Biotech & Health L25: \nL26: cite13†Climate L27: \nL28: cite14†Cloud Computing L29: \nL30: cite15†Commerce L31: \nL32: cite16†Crypto L33: \nL34: cite17†Enterprise L35: \nL36: cite18†EVs L37: \nL38: cite19†Fintech L39: \nL40: cite20†Fundraising L41: \nL42: cite21†Gadgets L43: \nL44: cite22†Gaming L45: \nL46: cite23†Google L47: \nL48: cite24†Government & Policy L49: \nL50: cite25†Hardware L51: \nL52: cite26†Instagram L53: \nL54: cite27†Layoffs L55: \nL56: cite28†Media & Entertainment L57: \nL58: cite29†Meta L59: \nL60: cite30†Microsoft L61: \nL62: cite31†Privacy L63: \nL64: cite32†Robotics L65: \nL66: cite4†Security L67: \nL68: cite33†Social L69: \nL70: cite34†Space L71: \nL72: cite1†Startups L73: \nL74: cite35†TikTok L75: \nL76: cite36†Transportation L77: \nL78: cite2†Venture L79: ### More from TechCrunch\nL80: \nL81: cite37†Staff L82: \nL83: cite8†Events L84: \nL85: cite38†Startup Battlefield L86: \nL87: cite39†StrictlyVC†strictlyvc.com L88: \nL89: cite10†Newsletters L90: \nL91: cite9†Podcasts L92: \nL93: cite40†Videos L94: \nL95: cite41†Partner Content L96: \nL97: cite42†TechCrunch Brand Studio L98: \nL99: cite43†Contact Us L100: \nL101: cite44†Image: Nvidia founder and CEO Jensen Huang Image Credits:Fabrice COFFRINI/AFP / Getty Images\nL102: \nL103: cite5†AI L104: # Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang tells Trump ‘we’re not going to let [an AI slowdown] happen’\nL105: \nL106: cite45†Amanda Silberling L107: \nL108: 2:51 PM PDT · September 14, 2026\nL109: \nL110: While Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang was onstage at the All-In Summit on Monday morning in Los Angeles, he got a phone call that he couldn’t send to voicemail — it was from President Donald Trump.\nL111: “I’m onstage with the besties,” Huang told Trump, referring to the venture capitalists who host the cite46†podcast All-In†podcasts.apple.com , including tech investors Chamath Palihapitiya, Jason Calacanis, and cite47†White House advisers†www.whitehouse.gov David Friedberg and cite48†David Sacks .\nL112: \nL113: In a move that will spark night terrors for public relations professionals everywhere, Huang flaunted his comfort with the president by putting Trump on speaker for conference attendees to hear.\nL114: > Holy crap POTUS just phoned in cite49†@JensenHuang†x.com live on stage at All In Summit\nL115: > We will not lose the ai race! And whatever Dario said this weekend won’t stop our progress\nL116: > This made my morning!cite50†$NVDA†x.com cite51†pic.twitter.com/Sr4wV9kMfz†t.co L117: >\nL118: > — Ben Pouladian ✈️ All-in Summit (@benitoz) cite52†September 14, 2026†x.com L119: “The great thing about life is Jensen can develop the most complex computer chip in the world … but he can’t figure out how to put you on speaker,” Trump said on the phone. (Huang is likely aware of how speakerphones work; he was asking stagehands for an extra microphone so that he could project the call to the audience.)\nL120: Onstage, Huang and the All-In hosts had been discussing Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei’s call to “cite53†slow the pace†darioamodei.com at which we improve the capabilities of AI.” SpaceX CEO Elon Musk and OpenAI CEO Sam Altman have publicly said they agree with Amodei, while Huang’s views have diverged.\nL121: “They’re just playing right in the hands of a lot of people that don’t want to see it happen,” Trump said. “That could be political people. It could also be China. And we’re not going to let that happen. It’s a hoax.”\nL122: \nL123: “You’re right. We’re not going to let that happen, sir,” Huang replied as the crowd broke into applause.\nL124: Trump and his allies, like Y Combinator CEO Garry Tan, have stated their belief that the growing sentiment against data center construction is part of an cite54†international psyop†garryslist.org to stifle American economic growth. But the source of the cite55†public’s concern†www.theverge.com is more straightforward.\nL125: Recent Gallup polling shows that seven in 10 Americans cite56†oppose the construction of data centers in their area†news.gallup.com , with more than 50% of respondents citing the effect of data centers on cite57†environmental resources . About 20% said they were concerned about cost-of-living increases and effects on their quality of life.\nL126: “We have to be a little bit careful … We have to do things and we have to do them prudently, but that doesn’t mean we’re going to stop an industry,” Trump said to Huang. “So I’m with you all the way. I didn’t even know how you felt about it. I just hoped you felt the same way as me. We’re going to lead.”\nL127: \nL128: Topics\nL129: \nL130: cite5†AI , cite58†Donald Trump , cite24†Government & Policy , cite59†Jensen Huang , cite60†nvidia L131: When you purchase through links in our articles, cite61†we may earn a small commission . This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nL132: \nL133: cite62†Image: Amanda Silberling L134: \nL135: Amanda Silberling\nL136: \nL137: Senior Writer\nL138: Amanda Silberling is a senior writer at TechCrunch covering the intersection of technology and culture. She has also written for publications like Polygon, MTV, the Kenyon Review, NPR, and Business Insider. She is the co-host of Wow If True, a podcast about internet culture, with science fiction author Isabel J. Kim. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she worked as a grassroots organizer, museum educator, and film festival coordinator. She holds a B.A.\nL139: in English from the University of Pennsylvania and served as a Princeton in Asia Fellow in Laos.\nL140: You can contact or verify outreach from Amanda by emailing amanda@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at @amanda.100 on Signal.\nL141: \nL142: cite45†View Bio L143: \nL144: cite63†Image: Event Logo L145: \nL146: October 13 – 15\nL147: \nL148: San Francisco\nL149: \nL150: Last day to book an exhibit table is September 18. Don’t miss out on high-impact leads, investor access, and a brand spotlight in Disrupt’s Expo Hall.\nL151: \nL152: cite64†BOOK NOW L153: ## Most Popular\nL154: \nL155:   * ###\nL156: \nL157: cite65†Tesla says it will finally unveil the second-generation Roadster on October 1 L158: \nL159:     * cite66†Anthony Ha L160: \nL161:   * ###\nL162: \nL163: cite67†Revolut confirms customer data breach through fake government requests L164: \nL165:     * cite68†Jagmeet Singh L166: \nL167:   * ###\nL168: \nL169: cite69†OpenAI puts Pro subscriptions on hold due to Astra demand L170: \nL171:     * cite70†Sarah Perez L172: \nL173:   * ###\nL174: \nL175: cite71†Bending Spoons to buy collaboration tools maker Miro for $1.36B, 90% less than its 2022 valuation L176: \nL177:     * cite72†Ram Iyer L178: \nL179:   * ###\nL180: cite73†ID verification giant IDScan confirms data breach with more than 150 million driver’s licenses stolen L181: \nL182:     * cite74†Zack Whittaker L183: \nL184:   * ###\nL185: \nL186: cite75†Automattic’s board forces CEO Matt Mullenweg into leave of absence L187: \nL188:     * cite76†Julie Bort L189:     * cite70†Sarah Perez L190: \nL191:   * ###\nL192: \nL193: cite77†‘Gambling with our lives’: Anthropic researcher quits, warns against self-improving AI  L194: \nL195:     * cite78†Rebecca Bellan L196: \nL197: Loading the next article\nL198: \nL199: Error loading the next article\nL200:   * cite79†TechCrunch L201:   * cite37†Staff L202:   * cite43†Contact Us L203:   * cite80†Advertise L204:   * cite81†Site Map L205: \nL206:   * cite82†Terms of Service L207:   * cite83†Privacy Policy L208:   * cite84†RSS Terms of Use L209:   * cite85†Code of Conduct L210: \nL211:   * cite86†OpenAI L212:   * cite87†Hugging Face L213:   * cite88†Flock L214:   * cite89†Startup Battlefield L215:   * cite7†Disrupt 2026 L216:   * cite90†Tech Layoffs L217:   * cite91†ChatGPT L218: \nL219: © 2026 TechCrunch Media LLC.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTrump rejects calls to slow AI development, citing Chinese competition - The Washington Post (https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/09/13/trump-rejects-calls-so-slow-ai-development-citing-chinese-competition/)\nciteturn792view1 [wordlim: 25] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn791search5\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 277\nL0: cite0†Accessibility statement cite1†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: cite2†Democracy Dies in Darkness L3: \nL4: Democracy Dies in Darkness\nL5: # Trump resists AI slowdown as the political tide turns\nL6: \nL7: Democrats are increasingly attacking the administration’s hands-off approach to artificial intelligence regulation as midterms approach.\nL8: \nL9: September 13, 2026 at 5:10 p.m. EDT September 13, 2026\nL10: \nL11: 9 min\nL12: \nL13: Summary\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Make us preferred on Google†www.google.com L16: President Donald Trump on Sunday at the Irish Open golf tournament, in Doonbeg, Ireland, where he responded to calls for slowing the development of artificial intelligence. (Paul Faith/AFP/Getty Images)\nL17: \nL18: By cite4†Cat Zakrzewski L19: \nL20: ,\nL21: \nL22: cite5†Violet Jira L23: \nL24: and\nL25: \nL26: cite6†Nitasha Tiku L27: DUBLIN — President Donald Trump on Sunday appeared to dismiss calls from top artificial intelligence leaders to slow the development of AI models, as Democrats seize on his administration’s resistance to regulation as a line of attack in the midterms.\nL28: \nL29: “We’re leading China in AI, we’re the most sophisticated country in the world, and frankly I want to keep it that way,” he said on the sidelines of the Irish Open golf tournament. “Whoever wins AI wins.”\nL30: Trump has staked much of his economic record on speeding up AI advances, as a boom in the technology drives historic highs in the stock market. But that hands-off approach is facing new scrutiny after three of the AI industry’s most prominent leaders — Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman and xAI founder Elon Musk — cite7†endorsed slowing the pace at which the industry improves AI model capabilities.\nL31: Their united front followed the resignation of an Anthropic researcher cite8†who on Tuesday warned that the firm and its competitors were not responsibly developing AI models. The researcher’s statement inflamed public debate about the risks of the technology.\nL32: On Sunday afternoon, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella said his company welcomed ideas like deliberate pacing and embedding evaluators, with some caveats. In a statement posted on X, Nadella emphasized that any safety regimen will also have to factor in enterprise customers’ need for privacy and flexibility. “[F]or firms, it’s imperative that they retain full control over their unique and tacit knowledge,” he wrote.\nL33: Trump’s remarks placed him once again firmly on the full-speed-ahead side of the debate over the pace of AI development, which has increasingly cite9†divided both the technology and political worlds . He faces growing isolation in his position, as even members of his own party and some allies in the tech industry have grown uneasy with Trump’s apparent lack of concern over potential AI dangers.\nL34: \nL35: Trump attributed the warnings to unspecified “negative forces.”\nL36: “I think you have a lot of negative forces that are bringing it up that shouldn’t be bringing it up,” he told reporters. “And they’re bringing up things that won’t happen.”\nL37: \nL38: Many hours later, on the flight from Ireland back to Washington, Trump insisted he was not downplaying the tech executives’ warning and said he had spoken with some of them.\nL39: \nL40: “I’m not downplaying. But it’s going to be more good than bad — by a lot,” he said, referring to AI. He also said that he has used AI but declined to say how.\nL41: Democrats are seeking to capitalize on growing public unease about AI, with multiple polls showing Americans increasingly wary of the technology and its rapid development. The issue potentially ties together several Democratic lines of attack, allowing them to portray Trump as inattentive to his work and too close to billionaire leaders of the tech industry.\nL42: In a flurry of social media posts over the weekend, Democrats spanning the party’s ideological spectrum pledged to rein in the industry and accused the administration of failing to confront the technology’s mounting risks.\nL43: \nL44: Abdul El-Sayed, a progressive Democratic Senate candidate in Michigan, warned Sunday that technological development was outpacing safety practices and endorsed a proposal from Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-Vermont) to cite10†pause AI development .\nL45: “There are things that we should not be using AI to do, and we collectively have to decide that we need public oversight so that all of this technology is not being created for the interest of a few against the well-being of the many,” El-Sayed said in an interview on “Face the Nation.”\nL46: \nL47: Josh Shapiro, the Pennsylvania governor, a centrist and potential 2028 Democratic presidential candidate, called the industry leaders’ warnings a “bright flashing red light.”\nL48: “It’s long past time for our federal government to step up, take this seriously, and put up some guardrails — both here at home and on the global stage,” Shapiro cite11†wrote on X†x.com , drawing parallels to the regulation of pharmaceuticals and airplanes.\nL49: \nL50: Shapiro recently has grown critical of the industry after previously fostering construction of the data centers underpinning AI development. Last month, he cite12†signed an executive order restricting their development.\nL51: Former president Barack Obama last week also privately urged Democratic Party leaders to make AI regulation central to the party’s agenda. He encouraged them to focus on safety, the technology’s impact on children and its economic impacts.\nL52: Obama viewed the industry leaders’ calls for slowing down AI development as a “positive development,” according to a person familiar with his thinking, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to describe private conversations. The New York Times first cite13†reported†www.nytimes.com Obama’s recommendation.\nL53: \nL54: The Democratic attacks have posed a challenge for Republicans, who are increasingly aware of the voter backlash to AI but are running on a party agenda that is inextricably tied to the White House.\nL55: Trump has moved to clear regulations that his donors in the tech industry warned could slow AI development down. During his first week in office, he rescinded cite14†a 2023 Biden executive order that created a process for evaluating the risks of cutting-edge AI models. As concerns about AI risks have grown this summer, the administration has cite15†developed a new framework for reviewing AI models for cybersecurity risks.\nL56: Some Republicans facing tight midterm races have expressed greater openness to AI regulation. Mike Rogers, a former Republican lawmaker who is battling El-Sayed for the Senate seat in Michigan, wrote on X on Sunday that he supports slowing down AI development and would prioritize passing a national security framework addressing the technology.\nL57: “My experience as Intel Chairman taught me the time to stop a crisis is yesterday,” said Rogers, who served as chairman of the House Intelligence Committee from 2011 to 2015.\nL58: \nL59: Republican leaders have sought to place the onus on companies to protect the public, doubling down on the White House’s position.\nL60: “There is an obvious corporate responsibility that the people who are creating these models have to have to ensure that their products are safe,” House Speaker Mike Johnson (R-Louisiana) said Sunday on CNN.\nL61: Johnson said he would meet with the leaders of the tech platforms as a first step. He also pointed to a post from David Sacks, the architect of the Trump administration’s hands-off approach to AI regulation. Tech leaders should “go ahead” with pacing AI development, Sacks said, but he rejected the companies’ claims that the industry needed intervention from Washington to do that.\nL62: \nL63: “Stop pretending you need anyone else’s permission,” Sacks cite16†wrote on X†x.com .\nL64: He also said that the companies should stop saying their interest in slowing down “is purely altruistic.” The companies would be liable if their products caused a significant cyberattack, he said.\nL65: \nL66: Amodei and other company officials have asked the U.S. government to waive antitrust laws so that they can coordinate on matters of AI safety, and Amodei wrote in his essay that it would be helpful if the government would “mediate or at least enable these discussions.”\nL67: Some policy experts and tech leaders dismissed the call for an antitrust exemption, arguing the companies were advocating policies that were favorable to their financial interests but would not further the goal of protecting public safety.\nL68: Slade Bond, a former Biden Justice Department official, said courts for a century have allowed companies to cooperate for the purpose of public safety. Companies can’t invoke safety as pretext for suppressing competition for other, illegal reasons. But a deal between AI companies to prevent a cataclysmal event, such as a financial crisis induced by a bot swarm, would be viewed as pro-competitive under the Sherman Antitrust Act, an 1890 statute that undergirds U.S. competition policy.\nL69: Asking the government for a waiver suggests the companies are extremely risk-averse when it comes to potential liability, despite having massive market caps, legal teams and litigation budgets, said Bond, who was a lead counsel on the House antitrust investigation into the tech industry in 2019 and 2020.\nL70: \nL71: “They want a blank check,” he said. “They don’t like product liability, they don’t like copyright, and now they don’t like antitrust.”\nL72: Industry leaders have also urged the administration to cooperate with China to pace AI development. Altman encouraged Trump to develop a pact with China that would address the risk of losing control over an AI agent, while ensuring that the safety measures do not leave either country at a disadvantage.\nL73: \nL74: Ahead of Trump’s meeting with Chinese President Xi Jinping later this month, Altman appealed to the president’s very public desire for a Nobel Peace Prize.\nL75: “I think Presidents Trump and Xi would get the Nobel Peace Prize together if they could agree on something that should be easy to agree to, and it would be wonderful,” Altman told Fortune magazine in an interview published Saturday.\nL76: “Even if just the U.S. and China could agree on some shared standards and testing for development of this technology, I think that’d be a wonderful accomplishment that the two of them can deliver,” he said. Trump and Xi reached no major agreements on artificial intelligence during their May summit in Beijing.\nL77: The White House did not respond to a request for comment on whether it would hold any policy meetings in response to the tech leaders’ warnings. Kevin Hassett, the director of the National Economic Council, said on CNN on Sunday that he expected the White House to have meetings this week with top cybersecurity advisers to “think about what the next step is.”\nL78: \nL79: * * *\nL80: ## Company\nL81: \nL82:   * cite17†About The Post L83:   * cite18†Newsroom Policies & Standards L84:   * cite19†WP Creative Group L85:   * cite20†Workforce Demographics†washingtonpost.com L86:   * cite21†Careers†careers.washingtonpost.com L87:   * cite22†Media & Community Relations L88:   * cite0†Accessibility Statement L89: ## Sections\nL90: \nL91:   * cite23†Trending L92:   * cite24†Politics L93:   * cite25†Elections L94:   * cite26†Opinions L95:   * cite27†U.S. News L96:   * cite28†World L97:   * cite29†Style L98:   * cite30†Sports L99:   * cite31†Business L100:   * cite32†Climate L101:   * cite33†Well+Being L102:   * cite34†D.C., Md., & Va. L103:   * cite35†Obituaries L104:   * cite36†Weather L105:   * cite37†Arts & Entertainment L106:   * cite38†Recipes L107: ## Get The Post\nL108:   * cite39†WP Intelligence†wpintelligence.washingtonpost.com L109:   * cite40†Enterprise Subscriptions†subscription.washingtonpost.com L110:   * cite41†Manage Your Subscription L111:   * cite42†Become a Subscriber†subscribe.washingtonpost.com L112:   * cite43†Gift Subscriptions†subscribe.washingtonpost.com L113:   * cite44†Mobile & Apps†subscription.washingtonpost.com L114:   * cite45†Newsletters & Alerts†washingtonpost.com L115:   * cite46†Washington Post Live L116:   * cite47†Reprints & Permissions L117:   * cite48†Post Store†store.washingtonpost.com L118:   * cite49†Books & E-Books L119:   * cite50†Print Archives (Subscribers Only)†www.proquest.com L120:   * cite51†Today’s Paper L121:   * cite52†Public Notices L122